<MapContentLayout title="How to Map Multiple Locations for Free – Easy Guide" heading="How to Map Multiple Locations on One Map" description="Prepare a spreadsheet or address list, preview a CSV import, fix unmatched rows, and share a map with multiple locations." intro="If your locations already live in a spreadsheet, prepare the list before placing the first pin. Complete addresses help distinguish branches with similar names. Coordinates let you plot an exact entrance or rural location directly. The sample CSV includes both an address and coordinates for each location." kind="article" guide>
  <div class="mm-reading">
    <h2>Why Create a Map With Multiple Locations?</h2>
    <p>A map can show which library branch is nearest, where all the entrances to a festival are, or which pickup point serves each neighborhood. Keep the source list so you can check every location and maintain it as details change.</p>
    <h2>Different Ways to Add Multiple Locations</h2>
    <h3>Add Locations Manually</h3><p>Place a pin or enter coordinates in the <a href="/multiple-location-map-generator/">map generator</a>. When address search is enabled, you can also look up each place and review the full address before selecting it.</p>
    <h3>Paste Multiple Addresses</h3><p>Copy a single address column into <strong>Add Multiple Locations</strong>. Keep one address per line. To include a note, use <code>address | description</code>. For example:</p>
    <pre><code>{'New Delhi, India | Head office\nMumbai, India | Regional office\nBengaluru, India | Technology center'}</code></pre>
    <h3>Import Locations From CSV</h3>
    <p><a href="/examples/multiple-map-locations.csv" download>Download the sample CSV</a> and keep its header. In a spreadsheet, export as comma-separated CSV. Quote fields containing commas if you write the file by hand. The file must be under 128 KB and fit the remaining marker limit shown in the editor.</p>
    <pre><code>{'title,address,lat,lon,description,color\nDelhi,New Delhi India,28.6139,77.2090,Capital of India,#e63946'}</code></pre>
    <p>Rows with both coordinates are added directly. The headers <code>latitude</code> and <code>longitude</code> also work. Address-only rows use the configured search provider when bulk lookup is enabled. If lookup is unavailable or reaches its limit, enter coordinates in the affected rows. Colors can be named values such as <code>green</code> or six-digit hex values such as <code>#e63946</code>.</p>
    <h3>Add Locations Using Coordinates</h3><p>Use decimal degrees in latitude, longitude order. For example, <code>40.7532, -73.9822</code> places a pin in Manhattan. Keep the minus sign for west longitude or south latitude.</p>
    <h2>How to Map Multiple Locations Step by Step</h2>
    <ol><li>Give each row one location and remove empty spreadsheet rows.</li><li>Open the generator and select <strong>Import CSV</strong> or <strong>Add Multiple Locations</strong>.</li><li>Preview the rows. Correct invalid coordinates or incomplete addresses.</li><li>Select <strong>Add Locations</strong> and review the results. For ambiguous matches, pick the correct result. For unresolved rows, correct the address or enter coordinates, then retry.</li><li>Use <strong>Fit All Locations</strong> and compare the pins with your source list.</li></ol>
    <h2>How to Organize Multiple Map Markers</h2><p>Put the most useful branch first if you want the map to open there. Use the move buttons to reorder numbered pins. Choose a consistent color for each category and check a few popups against your source sheet. The <a href="/custom-map-with-multiple-pins/">marker styling guide</a> shows ways to make categories clear.</p>
    <h2>How to Share a Map With Multiple Locations</h2><p>Give the map a title that describes the locations, then save it and copy the public URL. Download the recovery file for later updates and store it separately from the shareable link.</p>
    <h2>How to Embed a Multiple Location Map</h2><p>Copy the iframe into a custom HTML block on your site. Test it alongside the page’s written address list so visitors have another way to find a branch. Check the narrow layout and keep responsive width enabled.</p>
    <h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2><ul><li>Include the city or postcode for chain businesses and common street names.</li><li>Use separate latitude and longitude columns. A number in just one field needs correction before import.</li><li>Confirm that your CSV exporter uses commas and keeps long descriptions in a single field.</li><li>Keep private addresses and credentials out of the public map.</li></ul>
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details><summary>Can a single CSV mix addresses and coordinates?</summary><p>Yes. Rows with both coordinates are plotted directly. When bulk address lookup is enabled, address-only rows can use search. Otherwise, they stay in the preview so you can supply coordinates.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What if one row cannot be found?</summary><p>Successful rows are added. The unresolved row remains in the import panel, where you can edit it and retry.</p></details>
  </div>
</MapContentLayout>
